WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:01.940
How does counting sort work?

2
00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:04.340
Use small integer keys as

3
00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:06.880
array indices, count their frequencies,

4
00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:08.840
and turn those counts into

5
00:00:08.840 --> 00:00:11.380
output positions. The labels travel

6
00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:12.840
with their records, naturally.

7
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.320
Our six records have keys

8
00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.160
from zero through three, so

9
00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:18.500
the range size is four.

10
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.460
The letters distinguish equal-key records;

11
00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:23.220
they are not extra sorting

12
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:23.560
keys.

13
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.980
Scan the input once and

14
00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:27.560
increment the counter for each

15
00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:29.980
key. We get frequencies one,

16
00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.760
one, two, two: one zero,

17
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:34.720
one one, two twos, and

18
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:35.940
two threes.

19
00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:38.000
Add each frequency to the

20
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.120
cumulative total before it. The

21
00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.820
cumulative counts become one, two,

22
00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:45.840
four, six; each says how

23
00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:47.000
many records have a key

24
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.240
at most that value.

25
00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:51.000
These are counts, not zero-based

26
00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.120
output indices. For a key

27
00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.080
v, decrement its cumulative count

28
00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:56.940
first, then use the new

29
00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:58.420
count as its output position.

30
00:00:59.020 --> 00:01:00.640
Scan the input from right

31
00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:02.340
to left. Three b goes

32
00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:04.300
to index five, then one

33
00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:06.200
goes to index one, and

34
00:01:06.282 --> 00:01:07.560
two b goes to index

35
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:08.040
three.

36
00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:11.800
Continue backwards: the earlier three

37
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:14.160
goes to index four, zero

38
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.360
goes to index zero, and

39
00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:18.000
two a goes to index

40
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.340
two. Both pairs of equal

41
00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.360
keys retain their input order.

42
00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.480
Why is that stable? A

43
00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:27.240
later equal record is processed

44
00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.180
first and receives the later

45
00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:31.840
available position. Earlier equals fill

46
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:34.060
earlier positions, preserving their relative

47
00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:34.420
order.

48
00:01:34.980 --> 00:01:36.840
Derive the time by counting

49
00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:39.600
the loops. Initialising and prefixing

50
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.300
the k counters costs order

51
00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:43.780
k; scanning n inputs and

52
00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:45.740
placing n records costs order

53
00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:47.760
n. Together that is theta

54
00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:49.060
of n plus k.

55
00:01:49.740 --> 00:01:51.620
The output buffer holds n

56
00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:54.179
records, and the count array

57
00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:56.440
holds k counters. Auxiliary space

58
00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:58.180
is order n plus k,

59
00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:00.740
so this stable implementation is

60
00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:02.380
not in-place under the course

61
00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:02.900
definition.

62
00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.460
Things to take note of.

63
00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:07.620
Linear time in n requires

64
00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:08.960
the range k to be

65
00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:10.600
at most proportional to n.

66
00:02:11.180 --> 00:02:12.840
Six records spread across a

67
00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:14.600
billion possible keys would be

68
00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:15.820
a rather wasteful choice.

69
00:02:16.300 --> 00:02:18.260
A known bounded negative range

70
00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:19.480
can be shifted to start

71
00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:22.338
at zero, but its width

72
00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.960
still determines storage. Empty input

73
00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:26.560
needs no placements; singleton input

74
00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:28.000
must still respect the chosen

75
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:28.720
range convention.

76
00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:31.380
Preserve whole records, choose one

77
00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.660
count convention, and scan backwards

78
00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:35.940
for this placement rule. Counting

79
00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:38.280
sort avoids the comparison-only lower

80
00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:40.440
bound by using integer indexing,

81
00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:42.380
not by magically doing fewer

82
00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:42.960
comparisons.
